  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 225 AM EST Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chilly conditions continue to start the week. \n\nTemperatures at or below freezing were observed this morning across \nmuch of the area including in portions of southeastern VA and \ninterior northeastern NC where the growing season was still active. \nTemperatures on the Eastern Shore did not quite reach freezing, but \nare two weeks past their median first freeze date. Therefore, the \ngrowing season has ended for everywhere except Norfolk, Portsmouth, \nVirginia Beach, Pasquotank County, Camden County, and Currituck \nCounty. Where the growing season has ended, NWS Wakefield will \nresume issuing Frost/Freeze products again in Spring 2027 when the \nnext growing season begins.\n\nAfternoon weather analysis indicates sfc high pressure centered over \nthe Mid-West and a positively tilted mid-level trough over the Mid-\nAtlantic and New England. Conditions this afternoon are cool and \ncloudy with temperatures in the mid 50s. Not quite as breezy today, \nbut still seeing a few gusts up to 20mph. Winds will die out and \nskies will gradually clear overnight as the sfc high starts to build \neast. Looks like another cold night will lows just below freezing \ninland and upper 30s immediately near the coast. Sunny skies, less \nwind, and temperatures a few degrees higher will make it feel a \nlittle warmer tomorrow than the last couple of days. Highs will be \nin the upper 50s. Monday night, though, looks like a good set up for \nradiational cooling as high pressure will be almost directly \noverhead. As a result, Monday night/Tuesday morning will likely\nbe cold with temps as low as the mid 20s inland, mid 30s right \nby the coast. Highs on Tuesday will be around 60. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Temperatures show a gradual warming trend mid-\nweek through the weekend. Remaining dry. \n\nHigh pressure remains largely in place through the end of the week. \nAloft, the trough looks to finally move offshore around mid week, \nthen a ridge builds in behind it. This will allow for a moderating \ntrend in temperatures through the weekend. Forecast highs are in the \nmid 60s for Wednesday, then upper 60s Thurs-Sat. Lows will likely \nstill drop into the 30s each morning. With dry high pressure in \ncharge, there doesn't look to be a relief from the dry spell anytime \nsoon.",
